\section{Introduction}
Atong (natively A$\cdot$tong; ISO 639-3 aot) is a Sino-Tibetan (Tibeto-Burman) language spoken in the Garo Hills and Khasi Hills of Meghalaya, India, and across the border in Netrokona District, Bangladesh. It is closely related to Garo, Koch, Rabha and Bodo and is classed as severely endangered. Search results turned up scholarly documentation but no school materials.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primer, textbook or other school material for Atong was found in the search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item A Dictionary of Atong (Seino van Breugel, De Gruyter Mouton, Pacific Linguistics 664, 2021), with Atong-English and English-Atong sections and semantic lexica: \url{https://oeaw.ac.at/resources/Record/9783110718003}
\item Library record for the same dictionary: \url{https://vufind.lib.tsu.ru/Record/libtsu.1013546/Details}
\item Review of van Breugel's work in Lingua Posnaniensis: \url{https://pressto.amu.edu.pl/index.php/linpo/article/download/35458/30597/75406}
\item Overview article on the language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atong_language_(Sino-Tibetan)}
\end{itemize}
Other works named in the search summaries but without a verified URL: van Breugel, \emph{A Grammar of Atong} (Brill, 2014); \emph{Atong Texts} (Brill, 2019); and the 2009 Atong-English dictionary (Tura Book Room).

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found in the search results.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found in the search results.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The documentation is academic. A curriculum would need to begin from the dictionary, grammar and texts of van Breugel and from community input. Orthography and teaching materials remain to be developed.
